# THE RULE THIS FILE FOLLOWS
#
# Every path in this repo falls into exactly one of three categories. Deciding
# which, before adding a service, is the point — the entries below are just the
# consequence.
#
#   1. COMMITTED CONFIG — non-secret settings that make the stack portable.
#      A .env holding ports, hostnames and image tags belongs in git: clone the
#      repo on another machine and it stands up the same way. Say so explicitly
#      in a comment when it is not obvious, because "a committed .env" looks
#      like a mistake to anyone reading quickly.
#
#   2. MACHINE-LOCAL STATE — real but not portable. Container data directories,
#      per-machine registrations, build artifacts. Ignored, and never a thing
#      another clone needs.
#
#   3. SECRETS — never committed, on any branch, ever. Unseal keys, service
#      account credentials, private keys, tokens. If one lands in history,
#      rotating the secret is the fix; deleting the file is not.
#
# Add each new entry under the heading it belongs to, with a reason if the
# reason is not obvious from the path.

# --- 2. Machine-local state -------------------------------------------------

# Python tooling
.venv/
__pycache__/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
*.swp

# CloudStack installer checkpoints and logs. These land at the repo root rather
# than /var/lib because the vendored installer sets CS_STATE_DIR relative to its
# own location — two levels up from cloudstack/scripts/.
cloudstack-installer-tracker.conf
cloudstack-installer.log
installer.log

# CoreDNS. Both files here are generated by coredns-installer.sh from addresses
# discovered on this host, which makes them category 2, not category 1. The zone
# is rendered from lab.test.zone.tmpl because a DNS zone file has no variable
# syntax. The .env holds only CLOUDBR0_IP and GATEWAY_IP — the one genuinely
# portable value, the image tag, is defaulted inline in docker-compose.yml — so
# "clone the repo and compose works" never applied to it: a committed .env
# carries the previous host's bridge address. The ${VAR:?} guards in
# docker-compose.yml make a missing value refuse rather than bind 0.0.0.0.
# Neither file is secret.
docker/coredns/.env
docker/coredns/zones/*.zone

# --- 3. Secrets -------------------------------------------------------------

# Belt and braces: catch common secret-shaped names anywhere in the tree, so a
# new service directory is protected before its specific rule is written.
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*.key.enc
*_rsa
*.tfvars

# The offline root CA (Phase 2.3). Generated once per lab by ca/scripts/root-ca-create.sh,
# and never committed: the key is the lab's trust anchor, and the certificate is
# generated per host, so a committed one would be stale on every other machine —
# the same reason the CoreDNS zone above is ignored rather than checked in.
#
# The key is named .key.enc to say out loud that it is encrypted, which means the
# *.key rule does not match it. These directory entries used to be the only thing
# protecting it, and that was a mistake: renaming pki/ to ca/ moved the rules and
# left the generated key behind, un-ignored, in a directory git could not read.
# `*.key.enc` above is the belt to this file's braces — it follows the key
# wherever a later rename puts it. `make lint` asserts both still match.
ca/root/
ca/intermediate/

# Vault's bootstrap certificate and key (Phase 2.4). Lives with the service that
# reads it rather than under ca/ — see 2.4-5 — and the key is unencrypted by
# necessity, because Vault starts unattended. That makes this the first private
# key in the tree that a copy of the tree actually exposes, so it is listed
# before the directory exists rather than after.
#
# Only Vault. Every other certificate in the lab is issued by Vault's own PKI
# engine after 3.4 (see 3.4-1), so no other service has a key here to ignore.
docker/vault/certs/
